1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
Body odor — if you notice a sour, sharp smell the morning after whiskey or wine, your body is still working through what you drank.

2
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:08,800
There are three main reasons.

3
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:13,280
How alcohol breaks down, what you drank, and your own body chemistry.

4
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,040
First, the breakdown process.

5
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:19,920
Your liver converts alcohol into acetaldehyde before turning it into acetic acid.

6
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:28,640
Acetaldehyde itself carries a sharp, sour smell, and part of it escapes through your breath and your skin before your body finishes processing it.

7
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:30,400
Second, the drink itself.

8
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:40,160
Whiskey, wine, and other dark spirits carry far more congeners than clear spirits like vodka — bourbon alone holds dozens of times more than vodka does.

9
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:44,160
Those extra compounds take longer to break down, so the smell lingers.

10
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:46,320
Third, your body chemistry.

11
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:56,880
Many people, especially of East Asian background, carry a weaker version of the enzyme that clears acetaldehyde, so it stays in the system longer even at the same amount consumed.

12
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:58,880
Sweating it out won't speed things up.

13
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:02,960
Your liver processes roughly one drink an hour, no faster.

14
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:06,800
Staying hydrated and giving it time is what actually works.
